# Built from the publisher's revised template example.
BOOK = 'Revelation'
TESTAMENT = 'New Testament'
GENRE = 'revelation'
SUBTITLE = 'The Revelation of Jesus Christ, Israel’s Future, and the Triumph of the Lamb'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Revelation is “the revelation of Jesus Christ” given to <b>John</b> (Rev. 1:1), who was on the island of Patmos “for the word of God, and for the testimony of Jesus Christ” (Rev. 1:9). The book is addressed to seven churches in Asia (Rev. 1:4, 11) and reveals future events including judgments, the rise of the Beast, the return of Christ, the kingdom, and the new creation.'), ('p', 'Revelation belongs to <b>Israel’s Prophecy Program</b>, not the Body of Christ. It fulfills prophetic promises given to Israel (Rev. 1:7; Zech. 12:10), describes Daniel’s 70th week (Rev. 11–13; Dan. 9:27), and culminates in Christ’s return to earth to reign (Rev. 19:11–16; Rev. 20:4–6).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Revelation', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how the visions progress, how the judgments unfold, and how the narrative builds toward Christ’s return and the new creation. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Vision of Christ and Messages to the Churches (Chs. 1–3)'), ('p', 'John sees the risen Christ (Rev. 1:12–18). Christ addresses seven churches with commendations and warnings (Rev. 2–3).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Throne Room and the Seals (Chs. 4–7)'), ('p', 'John is caught up to heaven (Rev. 4:1–2). The Lamb opens seven seals (Rev. 6), leading to judgments and the sealing of 144,000 Israelites (Rev. 7:1–8).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Trumpets, Woes, and the Rise of the Beast (Chs. 8–13)'), ('p', 'Seven trumpets sound (Rev. 8–9). The two witnesses prophesy (Rev. 11:3–12). The Beast and False Prophet rise (Rev. 13).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Bowls of Wrath and the Fall of Babylon (Chs. 14–18)'), ('p', 'The Lamb stands on Mount Zion (Rev. 14:1). Seven bowls of wrath are poured out (Rev. 16). Babylon falls (Rev. 17–18).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — The Return of Christ and the Kingdom (Chs. 19–20)'), ('p', 'Christ returns on a white horse (Rev. 19:11–16). Satan is bound (Rev. 20:1–3). The saints reign with Christ for 1,000 years (Rev. 20:4–6).'), ('h3', 'Movement 6 — The New Heaven and New Earth (Chs. 21–22)'), ('p', 'God creates a new heaven and new earth (Rev. 21:1). The New Jerusalem descends (Rev. 21:2). Christ promises, “Surely I come quickly” (Rev. 22:20).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Revelation', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Revelation should be understood within Israel’s prophetic program. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants readers to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Revelation of Jesus Christ</b> — Christ is revealed in glory (Rev. 1:1; 1:12–18).', '<b>Judgment and Justice</b> — God judges the earth (Rev. 6–16).', '<b>The Triumph of the Lamb</b> — The Lamb overcomes all enemies (Rev. 17:14).', '<b>The Kingdom</b> — Christ reigns for 1,000 years (Rev. 20:4–6).', '<b>The New Creation</b> — God makes all things new (Rev. 21:1–5).', '<b>Prophetic Fulfillment</b> — Every eye sees Him (Rev. 1:7; Zech. 12:10).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Revelation', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — who He is, what He has done, and how He is revealed in glory. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered focus before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these verses together, you get a clear picture of how Revelation presents Christ as Judge, King, Lamb, and Lord of all.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Alpha and Omega</b> — Christ declares, “I am Alpha and Omega” (Rev. 1:8).', '<b>The Lamb</b> — Christ is the Lamb slain and worthy (Rev. 5:6–12).', '<b>The Faithful and True Judge</b> — Christ judges in righteousness (Rev. 19:11).', '<b>King of Kings</b> — Christ returns as King of kings and Lord of lords (Rev. 19:16).', '<b>The One Who Lives Forever</b> — “I am he that liveth, and was dead” (Rev. 1:18).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Revelation', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Revelation speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Revelation operates within Israel’s prophetic framework, fulfilling promises made to the nation. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Revelation in its own setting — as the capstone of Israel’s prophecy — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('ul', ['The 144,000 are Israelites sealed from twelve tribes (Rev. 7:4–8).', 'The temple is measured (Rev. 11:1), showing Israel’s covenant context.', 'The two witnesses prophesy in Jerusalem (Rev. 11:3–8).', 'Christ returns to Israel (Rev. 1:7; Zech. 12:10).', 'The Beast persecutes Israel (Rev. 12:13–17).', 'The book does <b>not</b> address the Body of Christ’s heavenly calling.'])]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Revelation that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Revelation contributes to Israel’s prophetic story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['Prophetic fulfillment: “Every eye shall see him” (Rev. 1:7).', 'Israel’s preservation in the wilderness (Rev. 12:6, 14).', 'The sealing of 144,000 Israelites (Rev. 7:4–8).', 'The temple and Jerusalem central to events (Rev. 11:1–8).', 'Christ’s earthly kingdom (Rev. 20:4–6).', 'The New Jerusalem as Israel’s promised city (Rev. 21:2, 12).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Revelation Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Revelation and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on the prophetic setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Revelation teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Revelation', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['Christ Revealed', 'Christ appears in glory', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Rev. 1:12–18'], ['Prophetic Fulfillment', 'Every eye shall see Him', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Rev. 1:7'], ['Judgments', 'Seals, trumpets, bowls', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Rev. 6–16'], ['144,000', 'Israelites sealed', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Rev. 7:4–8'], ['Two Witnesses', 'Prophets in Jerusalem', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Rev. 11:3–12'], ['Beast &amp; False Prophet', 'Rise of Antichrist', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Rev. 13'], ['Kingdom', 'Christ reigns 1,000 years', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Rev. 20:4–6']], [20, 38, 26, 16])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Revelation that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s prophetic instructions, this section helps you see what Revelation teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Christ is Lord', 'Christ is King of kings', 'Rev. 19:16'], ['God Judges Sin', 'God’s judgments are righteous', 'Rev. 16:7'], ['Worship God Alone', '“Worship God”', 'Rev. 22:9'], ['God Makes All Things New', 'New creation', 'Rev. 21:5'], ['Christ Returns', 'Christ comes quickly', 'Rev. 22:20']], [26, 50, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Revelation without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s prophetic story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing Israel-program commands for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Revelation in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Worship Christ</b> — Christ is worthy (Rev. 5:12).', '<b>Trust God’s Justice</b> — God’s judgments are righteous (Rev. 16:7).', '<b>Reject Idolatry</b> — Worship God alone (Rev. 22:9).', '<b>Live in Hope</b> — Christ comes quickly (Rev. 22:20).', '<b>Rest in God’s Sovereignty</b> — The Lamb overcomes (Rev. 17:14).', '<b>Remember God’s Faithfulness</b> — God makes all things new (Rev. 21:5).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Revelation', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Revelation in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Vision of Christ (Rev. 1)', 'Messages to the Churches (Rev. 2–3)', 'Throne Room &amp; Seals (Rev. 4–7)', 'Trumpets, Woes, &amp; the Beast (Rev. 8–13)', 'Bowls &amp; Babylon (Rev. 14–18)', 'Christ’s Return &amp; the Kingdom (Rev. 19–20)', 'New Heaven &amp; New Earth (Rev. 21–22)'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Revelation, helping you see both what the book says and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications draw only on the universal truths Revelation itself states, so your response stays tied to the text.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Revelation simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group see what the book states and what it leaves unsaid, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Revelation is read on the book’s own audience markers and setting. This Companion shows you how to read the book in its own setting while drawing out the universal truths it states.')])]

